---
import TutorialLayout from '../../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro';
import ModelWorkbench from '../../../components/tutorials/ModelWorkbench';
import MathBlock from '../../../components/common/MathBlock.astro';
---

<TutorialLayout
  title="Series RLC Circuit"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Getting Started', href: '/tutorials/getting-started' },
    { label: 'Series RLC' },
  ]}
  prev={{ label: 'Van der Pol', href: '/tutorials/getting-started/van-der-pol' }}
  next={{ label: 'Circuit Components', href: '/tutorials/getting-started/circuit-components' }}
>
  <div class="mb-6 p-4 rounded-lg border border-accent/20 bg-accent/5">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-accent mb-2">What you'll learn</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-sm text-text-muted space-y-1">
      <li>How electrical circuits map to the same ODE structures as mechanical systems</li>
      <li>Underdamped oscillation in a different physical domain</li>
      <li>The effect of resistance, inductance, and capacitance on transient response</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    A series RLC circuit driven by a step voltage source. The inductor and capacitor trade energy back
    and forth, producing oscillating current and voltage &mdash; the electrical analogue of the
    mass-spring-damper. The resistor dissipates energy, damping the oscillation over time.
  </p>

  <MathBlock tex={`\\begin{aligned}
L\\,\\frac{di_L}{dt} &= V_0 - R\\,i_L - V_c \\\\
C\\,\\frac{dV_c}{dt} &= i_L
\\end{aligned}`} />

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Notice this is the same structure as the mass-spring-damper: inductance plays the role of mass,
    capacitance is the inverse of stiffness, and resistance is damping. Simulate to see the transient response.
  </p>

  <ModelWorkbench
    client:only="react"
    initialSource={`model SeriesRLC "Series RLC circuit with step voltage"
  parameter Real R = 10.0 "Resistance (Ohm)";
  parameter Real L = 0.1 "Inductance (H)";
  parameter Real C = 1e-3 "Capacitance (F)";
  parameter Real V0 = 10.0 "Source voltage (V)";
  Real iL(start = 0.0) "Inductor current (A)";
  Real Vc(start = 0.0) "Capacitor voltage (V)";
equation
  L * der(iL) = V0 - R * iL - Vc;
  C * der(Vc) = iL;
end SeriesRLC;`}
    modelName="SeriesRLC"
    tEnd={0.5}
  />

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-10 mb-3">Things to try</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-2">
    <li>Increase <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">R</code> to <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">50</code> &mdash; the circuit becomes overdamped and the capacitor charges without ringing.</li>
    <li>Set <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">R = 0</code> for an ideal LC circuit &mdash; the oscillation never decays.</li>
    <li>Try <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">C = 1e-4</code> &mdash; smaller capacitance means faster oscillations (higher natural frequency).</li>
    <li>Compare the electrical analogy: <em>L</em> &harr; mass, <em>1/C</em> &harr; stiffness, <em>R</em> &harr; damping coefficient.</li>
  </ul>
</TutorialLayout>
